  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>elderabusecalc.com is a free informational resource about elder abuse, nursing home neglect, financial exploitation of older adults, and the legal framework governing these cases in the United States. The site exists to help patients, families, and other interested parties understand the rough scope of compensation that elder abuse cases produce &mdash; with enough specificity to inform initial decisions, while staying honest about the limits of general informational content.</p>

  <h2>A note on sensitive content</h2>

  <p>Many readers reach this site at a difficult moment &mdash; sometimes during an active abuse situation, sometimes after a parent or grandparent has died and the family is trying to understand what happened. We try to handle this carefully. We aim for content that is substantive without being clinical, and honest without being alarmist.</p>

  <p>If you believe abuse is happening right now, the priority is the older person&rsquo;s safety, not a lawsuit. Please contact:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emergency:</strong> Call 911.</li>
    <li><strong>National Center on Elder Abuse / Eldercare Locator:</strong> 1-800-677-1116. This line connects you to local resources including your state&rsquo;s Adult Protective Services intake.</li>
    <li><strong>State Adult Protective Services (APS):</strong> every state has an intake line, often staffed 24/7. The Eldercare Locator above will route you to it.</li>
    <li><strong>State Long-Term Care Ombudsman:</strong> for concerns specific to a nursing home or assisted living facility, your state ombudsman can investigate complaints. Find yours through the Eldercare Locator.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Civil legal claims can wait. Immediate protection cannot.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Provide a calculator that estimates recovery ranges based on abuse type, severity, state framework, and case-specific facts, using published settlement data and state Elder Abuse Act remedies.</li>
    <li>Explain how elder abuse cases are valued: damages categories, the role of CMS F-Tag evidence, state-statutory enhancements (attorney fees, treble damages, pre-death pain and suffering for heirs), and wrongful death overlays.</li>
    <li>Document the seven recognized categories of elder abuse and the common nursing home neglect patterns.</li>
    <li>Provide step-by-step guidance for what to do when abuse is suspected &mdash; APS reporting, evidence preservation, attorney consultation, family communication, and facility-management escalation.</li>
    <li>Address common misconceptions about elder abuse claims &mdash; including the under-appreciated importance of analyzing pre-dispute arbitration clauses in nursing home admission contracts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>The full mechanics &mdash; the damages categories, the state Elder Abuse Act enhancements, the CMS F-Tag evidence that supports a neglect claim, and two full worked examples &mdash; are documented on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. At a high level: the calculator starts from the damages categories common to nursing home neglect and elder abuse cases (past and future medical expenses, pain and suffering, restitution for financial exploitation, wrongful death and survival damages), then applies a state-framework modifier for the handful of states with unusually plaintiff-favorable statutes, and a severity modifier based on the type and extent of harm. The result is a range, not a prediction &mdash; two cases with similar facts can settle very differently depending on the facility&rsquo;s documentary record, insurance coverage, and the jurisdiction&rsquo;s procedural rules.</p>

  <h2>What we don&rsquo;t do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Provide legal advice. We are not attorneys.</li>
    <li>Provide medical advice. We are not medical providers.</li>
    <li>Provide social-work intervention. We are not Adult Protective Services or a substitute for it.</li>
    <li>Review individual cases or predict outcomes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The calculator itself collects nothing beyond standard analytics &mdash; it runs in your browser and we do not store what you enter. Separately, the homepage offers an optional case-review form that connects readers with attorneys; that <em>is</em> a disclosed paid referral, not editorial content, and using it is always optional. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for exactly what's collected and shared if you use it.</p>

  <h2>The team</h2>
  <p>The editorial team is a small group of elder law and long-term care paralegals at The Click Lab. Full details at <a href="/editorial/">the editorial team page</a>.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>This site is supported by display advertising via Google AdSense. We don&rsquo;t accept payment from nursing facilities or care providers for placement or a favorable mention in our editorial content, and our guides and methodology are not for sale. Separately, we offer an optional case-review form on the homepage. Depending on the details of your case, our team may connect you directly with an attorney in your area, or your information may be shared with our network of partner attorneys. You're never obligated to work with any attorney we connect you with, and there's no cost to you for this connection. That's a disclosed advertising product, separate from our editorial content, and using it is always optional. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details on data handling, including exactly what's shared if you use that form.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Editorial questions, corrections, or interview requests: <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We do not handle abuse reports; please contact APS or call the Eldercare Locator at 1-800-677-1116 for those.</p>
</BaseLayout>
